Track 1: Latitude 74 Eats Its Dead
- Story & Context: In the bleak midwinter of 1987, lighthouse relief operator Henrik Vance logged a rogue radio signal cutting through the Svalbard blizzard, blending a discarded 1952 fiddle record with the brutal pulse of an abandoned sonar rig. The transmission lured three Norwegian timber barges into the shoals, their captain claiming over shortwave that the groove was physically dragging their rudders toward the rocks. As the rescue tugs pulled the wreck clear, Henrik recorded the distorted maritime distress calls directly onto magnetic tape, loop-stitching them into a heavy, grinding dembow pulse that became the founding anthem of the genre. "Drop the anchor deep or the current takes the soul," Vance murmured into his dead-channel microphone as the signal finally faded into arctic ice.
