Track 2: Zemlya
- Story & Context: Named after the unmapped Franz Josef Land archipelago, this track was composed during a 40-day polar night when operator Maren O'Sullivan believed she was receiving telepathic code from an empty radar station across the Barents Sea. Armed only with her grandmother’s 1948 acoustic violin and an overloaded Korg synthesizer, she tracked this mournful character study of a ghost navigator who refused to return to land. The track’s emotional peak occurs when the heavy EBM bass drops out entirely, leaving a raw, unamplified fiddle note hanging over the ocean soundscapes like an unreturned signal. "No soil on my boots till the ocean forgets your face," sings a pitched-down vocal sample recorded through a 1950s carbon-capsule telephone handset.
